# Task #2 Completion Notes: Executor guard in buildSystemPrompt

## What was done

### TDD: Tests added first to `test/agent-prompt.test.ts`

Added two new tests in a `buildSystemPrompt` describe block:

1. **"injects specialist body for a non-internal agent"** — creates a normal agent file, calls `buildSystemPrompt`, asserts the body IS in the prompt and `SPECIALIST INSTRUCTIONS:` header is present. This test passed immediately (existing behavior correct).

2. **"does NOT inject specialist body for an internal agent, and emits a warning"** — creates an internal agent (`internal: true` in `AgentInfo`), calls `buildSystemPrompt`, asserts the body is NOT in the prompt, `SPECIALIST INSTRUCTIONS:` is not present, and `console.warn` was called once with the agent name. This test failed before implementation (red phase confirmed).

Also added imports for `buildSystemPrompt`, `SystemPromptInput`, `AgentInfo`, `RalphConfig`, and `spyOn` from `bun:test`.

### Implementation in `src/commands/run.ts` (~lines 60-83)

In the specialist injection block, added an `agentInfo.internal` guard:

```typescript
if (agentInfo.internal) {
  console.warn(`[ralph] Agent '${taskAgent}' is marked internal and cannot be used as a task specialist — falling back to generalist prompt.`);
} else {
  // ... existing file read + specialistSection assignment
}
```

Non-internal agents inject exactly as before. Internal agents get a warning and `specialistSection` stays empty, so the prompt falls back to the generalist template.

## Test results

All 5 tests in `test/agent-prompt.test.ts` pass. Full suite: 667 pass, 0 fail.
